/**
@mainpage Atomiverse -- Unified Molecular Simulation Platform

@tableofcontents

@section intro Overview

Atomiverse is a molecular simulation codebase written in modern C++ and CUDA.
It uses one staged execution model to express three families of workflows:
molecular dynamics, minimization, and self-consistent electronic-structure
calculations.

The project is built around a clear separation of responsibilities:

- the **user-facing data model** lives in `cpu::Frame`
- the **facade layer** compiles a staged execution plan
- the **CPU and CUDA backends** execute that plan with different storage models
- the **API reference** documents the exact classes, functions, and headers

All public examples and build instructions in this repository assume
**C++20 or later**.

@section docs Documentation Structure

The documentation is intentionally split into three layers. Each layer answers a
different kind of question.

- [User Guide](user-guide/index.md) -- how to build the project, choose examples,
  and understand the main workflows without assuming prior chemistry knowledge
- [Developer Manual](developer/index.md) -- how the repository is organized
  internally, why the facade/backend split exists, and how to extend the code
- [API Reference](api/index.md) -- where to find the exact classes, functions,
  headers, and generated reference pages

@section quickstart Start Here

If you are new to Atomiverse, read these pages first:

1. [Getting Started](user-guide/getting-started.md)
2. [Chapter 0: A Tour of the CPU Backend](tutorial/ch00-architecture.md)
3. [Tutorials Index](tutorial/index.md)

The tutorial track is organised in two arcs. The SCF arc (Chapters 1-4,
6, 8) starts with the toy SCF loop and adds RHF, UHF, UKS, AIMD, and
geometry optimization one layer at a time. The classical-MD arc
(Chapter 7) walks through pair potentials, bonded force fields,
neighbor lists, and ensemble thermostats on the CPU.

@section arch Architecture At A Glance

The shortest correct architectural summary is:

@code{.unparsed}
  cpu::Frame
      |
      v
  Driver + Plan + Phase enums        -- backend-agnostic orchestration
      |
      +--> cpu::Backend              -- zero-copy execution on host data
      |
      +--> CUDA Backend              -- materialize cuda::Frame, run staged GPU components,
                                       retrieve results back to cpu::Frame
@endcode

Two design rules matter more than anything else:

- host and device data are represented by different types
- components follow a strict `build() -> launch() -> destroy()` lifecycle

For the full explanation, read [Architecture](developer/architecture.md).
For contribution guidance, read [Extending Components](developer/extending-components.md).
The CPU-specific deep-dive is in [CPU Backend Internals](developer/cpu-internals.md),
and worked extension recipes for MD, the minimizer, and SCF inner-step
helpers are in [Extending CPU Subsystems](developer/extending-cpu-subsystems.md).
[MD Execution Model](developer/md-execution-model.md) explains one molecular-dynamics
timestep as a fixed sequence of named phases — the LAMMPS *Developer flow* analogue.

@section workflows Workflow Families

Atomiverse currently exposes four workflow families.

@subsection workflows_md Molecular Dynamics

`md::MD` executes a LAMMPS-style phase sequence. Typical components include
`VelocityVerlet`, `NeighborList`, `PairLJCut`, `NoseHoover`, `Thermo`, and `Recorder`.
The full phase sequence and the `(phase, order)` binding model are documented in
[MD Execution Model](developer/md-execution-model.md).

@subsection workflows_min Minimization

The CPU side uses the `cpu::minimizer::Minimize` preset, which takes
any `Optimizer`-role component (`LBFGS`, `CG`, …) plus an `Evaluator`
at the force phase, and attaches a `cpu::ForceConverged` termination
criterion in its constructor. The same wiring drives classical relaxation
(`PairLJCut` at force) and *ab initio* geometry optimization
(`SCFEvaluator` at force). The CUDA side mirrors this exactly:
`cuda::minimizer::Minimize` takes `SteepestDescent`, `CG`, or `LBFGS`
Optimizer components. Both are presets over the one loop node
(`cpu::Driver<P>` / `cuda::Driver<P>`) — there is no category
base class between them. Ask the driver for the verdict
(`opt.converged()`, `opt.stop_reason()`), never the optimizer.

@subsection workflows_mc Monte Carlo Sampling

The CPU side adds the `cpu::mc::MC` preset: a Mover component
(`TranslateMove`) proposes a trial move each sweep, the MD force
pipeline recomputes the trial energy, and the loop's `OutcomePolicy` —
`cpu::Metropolis`, attached by `MC`'s constructor — answers `Commit` or
`Rollback`. Accept/reject is not a termination, so it is a policy slot
of its own: a GCMC or REMD acceptance rule is a `set_outcome()` call,
not a new driver class.

@subsection workflows_scf Electronic Structure

Electronic-structure code exists in two complementary forms:

- the CPU concrete methods `cpu::scf::RHF` / `UHF` / `RKS` / `UKS` — each a
  `Driver<scf::Phase>` node in its own right, so `rhf.run(frame)` drives the
  self-consistency loop and nesting the same object at an outer force phase
  drives AIMD or geometry optimization
- a device-resident private RHF cycle (`internal::RhfScfCycle`) owned by
  `cuda::scf::SCF` component and driven by `cuda::Evaluate`

@code
cpu::scf::RHF rhf{cpu::scf::BasisSet::STO3G};
rhf.run(frame);                 // frame.meta.get(kEnergy) [eV]
@endcode

There is no bag named `cpu::scf::SCFSolver`, no `SCFConfig`, and no
method-agnostic `cpu::scf::SCF` — name the ansatz you mean.

The user guide explains when to choose each path. The developer manual explains
why both layers exist.

@section examples Example Programs

Representative entry points:

- `examples/01_scf.cpp` -- explicit SCF introduction example
- `examples/02_rhf.cpp` -- CPU RHF tutorial example
- `examples/02_rhf.cu` -- CUDA translation of the same RHF lesson
- `examples/03_uhf.cpp` -- UHF bond breaking and spin contamination
- `examples/04_uks.cpp` -- UKS / LSDA examples
- `examples/05_geom_opt.cpp` -- L-BFGS aspirin geometry optimization
- `examples/06_aimd.cpp` -- ab initio molecular dynamics on H2 (NVT)
- `examples/07_md_lj.cpp` -- classical Lennard-Jones MD (CPU, argon)
- `examples/07_md_lj.cu` -- CUDA twin of the same classical-MD lesson
- `examples/08_pw.cpp` -- plane-wave DFT for a periodic solid
- `examples/09_gpw.cpp` -- GPW DFT single point (water)

@section docsbuild Building The Docs

Generate the documentation with:

@code{.bash}
cmake -B build/docs -DATV_DOCS_ONLY=ON
cmake --build build/docs --target docs
@endcode
*/
